.TH TGMATH 7 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
tgmath \- type-generic math macros
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tgmath.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B <tgmath.h>
header provides a set of type-generic macros that wrap the functions
declared in
.B <math.h>
and (when available)
.BR <complex.h> .
Each macro accepts arguments of any real floating-point type
(\fBfloat\fR, \fBdouble\fR, or \fBlong double\fR) and dispatches
the call to the correctly-typed underlying function, eliminating
the need for the programmer to manually select between
.BR sinf (3),
.BR sin (3),
and
.BR sinl (3).
.PP
The header is specified by ISO C99 (and retained by C11 and C23).
In Substrate it is implemented using the C11
.B _Generic
selection expression.
.SS Type Selection
The dispatching rule applied by each macro is:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
If any argument belonging to the type-domain of the function has type
.B long double
(or is an expression of that type), the
.B l
suffix variant is called (e.g.\&
.BR sqrtl (3)).
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise, if any such argument has type
.B float
(or an expression of that type), the
.B f
suffix variant is called (e.g.\&
.BR sqrtf (3)).
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise the unsuffixed
.B double
variant is called (e.g.\&
.BR sqrt (3)).
.RE
.PP
The selection is implemented via a
.B _Generic
expression.  Because
.B _Generic
does not accept integer types as alternatives (and because integer
literals such as
.B 2
have type
.BR int ),
a common idiom is to use the
.I +0
trick when an integer value is intended to be treated as
.BR double :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
sin(x + 0)    /* promotes integer x to double */
sin(x + 0.0)  /* equivalent, explicit */
.fi
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, a plain cast suffices:
.B sin((double)n) .
.SS Integer Arguments
If all arguments are integer types, the C11 standard specifies that the
call shall be treated as if the arguments were converted to
.B double
and the unsuffixed (double) variant is called.
The Substrate implementation achieves this behaviour through the
.B +0
promotion embedded inside each macro expansion.
.SS Covered Functions
The following function families are covered by
.BR <tgmath.h> :
.TP
.B Trigonometric
.BR acos ,
.BR asin ,
.BR atan ,
.BR atan2 ,
.BR cos ,
.BR sin ,
.BR tan
.TP
.B Hyperbolic
.BR acosh ,
.BR asinh ,
.BR atanh ,
.BR cosh ,
.BR sinh ,
.BR tanh
.TP
.B Exponential and Logarithmic
.BR exp ,
.BR exp2 ,
.BR expm1 ,
.BR frexp ,
.BR ilogb ,
.BR ldexp ,
.BR log ,
.BR log10 ,
.BR log1p ,
.BR log2 ,
.BR logb ,
.BR scalbn ,
.BR scalbln
.TP
.B Power and Absolute Value
.BR cbrt ,
.BR fabs ,
.BR hypot ,
.BR pow ,
.BR sqrt
.TP
.B Rounding and Truncation
.BR ceil ,
.BR floor ,
.BR nearbyint ,
.BR rint ,
.BR lrint ,
.BR llrint ,
.BR round ,
.BR lround ,
.BR llround ,
.BR trunc
.TP
.B Remainder and Modulo
.BR fmod ,
.BR modf ,
.BR remainder ,
.BR remquo
.TP
.B Floating-Point Manipulation
.BR copysign ,
.BR nan ,
.BR nextafter ,
.BR nexttoward
.TP
.B Positive Difference, Max, Min, FMA
.BR fdim ,
.BR fmax ,
.BR fmin ,
.BR fma
.SS No Multiple Evaluation
Each macro is designed so that no argument expression is evaluated more
than once.
Under the C11
.B _Generic
mechanism only the
.I controlling expression
of the selection is evaluated at run-time; all non-selected branches
are discarded at compile-time without evaluation.
Thus side effects in arguments (e.g.\&
.BR i++
or a function call) occur exactly once, as expected.
.SS Floating-Point Environment Interaction
If a program wishes to observe or control the floating-point exception
flags while calling functions through
.BR <tgmath.h> ,
it should enable access to the floating-point environment with:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
.fi
.RE
.PP
See
.BR fenv (7)
for details.
.SS C++ Compatibility
In C++ the functions declared in
.B <cmath>
are overloaded on argument type, making type-generic dispatch
automatic.
The C++
.B <tgmath.h>
header is therefore either a no-op or simply includes
.B <cmath>
and
.BR <complex> .
The macro-based dispatch mechanism described here applies only to C.
.SS Complex Number Variants
ISO C99 specifies that, when
.B <complex.h>
is included, the type-generic macros also dispatch to the complex
variants (e.g.\&
.BR csinf (3),
.BR csin (3),
.BR csinl (3)).
The Substrate implementation does not yet provide
.BR <complex.h> ;
the complex dispatch paths are reserved for a future release.
.SH NOTES
Including
.B <tgmath.h>
implicitly includes both
.B <math.h>
and
.BR <complex.h>
(when the latter is available).
.PP
The type-generic macros are purely a syntactic convenience; there is no
performance overhead relative to calling the typed functions directly.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <tgmath.h>

int main(void) {
    float       xf = 2.0f;
    double      xd = 2.0;
    long double xl = 2.0L;

    /* Each call dispatches to the appropriately-typed variant */
    float       r1 = sqrt(xf);   /* calls sqrtf(xf)  */
    double      r2 = sqrt(xd);   /* calls sqrt(xd)   */
    long double r3 = sqrt(xl);   /* calls sqrtl(xl)  */

    /* Integer argument — promoted to double via +0 trick */
    double r4 = sin(1);           /* calls sin(1+0) -> sin(double) */

    /* Explicit cast is equally valid */
    double r5 = sin((double)1);

    (void)r1; (void)r2; (void)r3; (void)r4; (void)r5;
    return 0;
}
.fi
